1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
How do you build creature comforts into a tower of steel and glass?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
From freight hoists to high-speed pressurized elevators.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
From ice to nice.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
In Los Angeles, we're constructing a mega building from the ground up.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
And we'll use it to explore the development of the systems that make the high life comfortable

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,000
while meeting the people who design them.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:31,100
them. Now, Building a Skyscraper, The Human Environment, on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:52,540
For the past year, eight months, and 20 days, the builders of the new California Department

9
00:00:52,541 --> 00:00:55,640
of Transportation Headquarters in downtown Los Angeles

10
00:00:55,641 --> 00:01:00,480
have been busy pouring concrete, erecting steel,

11
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:02,700
and installing walls and windows.

12
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:07,180
The massive 750,000-square-foot office building

13
00:01:07,181 --> 00:01:09,540
looks almost completed from the outside,

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,820
but the structure is still pretty naked on the inside.

15
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,660
It'll take another seven months to finish installing

16
00:01:18,661 --> 00:01:21,260
the heating, ventilation, and air conditioning system

17
00:01:21,261 --> 00:01:22,960
and elevators.

18
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:28,560
These are the things that make working in a large office building possible,

19
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:30,180
even bearable.

20
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:34,520
Beyond the introduction of steel cage construction,

21
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:38,080
no invention helped buildings, including our own,

22
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:40,220
climb higher than the elevator.

23
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:47,300
If we did not have passenger elevators,

24
00:01:47,301 --> 00:01:50,560
we don't have the modern multi-story office building

25
00:01:50,561 --> 00:01:56,000
because people won't walk more than two or three floors off the street to easily reach an office.

26
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:03,020
The Caltrans building will have 19 elevators to shuttle its 2,000 employees to upper floors.

27
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,220
Kone Incorporated will soon begin installing them.

28
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:15,560
But in the meantime, Clark Construction is running a temporary man lift to move workers and materials.

29
00:02:15,561 --> 00:02:21,560
At the helm, Bobby Jeffers, man lift operator extraordinaire.

30
00:02:21,561 --> 00:02:26,560
Yeah, you see, you guys, this is a man lift operator's life.

31
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:30,560
You're always getting loaded and you're always having your ups and downs.

32
00:02:30,561 --> 00:02:36,560
I've been running the man lift for 25 years all over Southern California.

33
00:02:36,561 --> 00:02:38,560
I've built hundreds of buildings.

34
00:02:38,561 --> 00:02:42,560
I'm the main person that brings everybody up.

35
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:44,560
The job won't run without me.

36
00:02:44,561 --> 00:02:51,560
The site uses an operator for a more efficient flow of materials.

37
00:02:51,561 --> 00:02:52,560
Okay.

38
00:02:52,561 --> 00:02:56,560
If I take you down, can I get a nice smile out of you guys?

39
00:02:56,561 --> 00:02:57,560
You ready?

40
00:02:57,561 --> 00:02:58,560
Okay, hang on.

41
00:02:58,561 --> 00:02:59,560
Hang on, baby.

42
00:02:59,561 --> 00:03:04,560
Bobby decides which subcontractor is performing a more crucial function at the time.

43
00:03:04,561 --> 00:03:05,560
That's right.

44
00:03:05,561 --> 00:03:07,560
a more crucial function at the time.

45
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:09,060
We all have to win our turn.

46
00:03:09,061 --> 00:03:10,060
That's right.

47
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,060
You guys going up or down?

48
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:18,060
Bobby prioritizes the pickups, but no one cares,

49
00:03:18,061 --> 00:03:21,060
because he's also assumed the role of site stand-up comedian.

50
00:03:21,061 --> 00:03:22,560
I haven't eaten in three days.

51
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:26,060
I've been on a liquid diet, beer and wine.

52
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:28,060
Where are you going, guys?

53
00:03:28,061 --> 00:03:29,060
Six.

54
00:03:29,061 --> 00:03:31,560
Bobby's man lift will only operate

55
00:03:31,561 --> 00:03:34,060
until the first two permanent elevators are running.

56
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:39,360
Like most of the elevators used in high-rises,

57
00:03:39,361 --> 00:03:42,440
these are what's known as roped traction elevators.

58
00:03:42,441 --> 00:03:45,280
There's the elevator car.

59
00:03:45,281 --> 00:03:50,440
Cables are attached to the car,

60
00:03:50,441 --> 00:03:54,560
go up to the top of the shaft and then over a pulley or shiv,

61
00:03:54,561 --> 00:03:59,000
and then back down the shaft to a set of counterweights that balance the car.

62
00:03:59,001 --> 00:04:02,000
Attached to the shiv is an electric motor,

63
00:04:02,001 --> 00:04:07,140
And when it operates, it turns the shiv or pulley, and then the car goes up or down the shaft.

64
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,720
Okay, we are in the pit. This is the bottom of the elevator.

65
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:21,320
At the Caltrans site, Ron Caborn with Kone Incorporated is responsible for getting all 19 elevators up and running.

66
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:28,960
The first step in the installation process is to attach the guide rails to the sides of the shaft.

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:44,600
Our main part of the elevator is our guide rails.

68
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:47,880
This is what the elevator travels on.

69
00:04:47,881 --> 00:04:56,840
It's very important that the guide rails are plumb.

70
00:04:56,841 --> 00:05:01,000
Anything crooked in the rail, the faster the car travels the more you'll feel it.

71
00:05:01,001 --> 00:05:03,400
These cars travel 500 foot a minute.

72
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:06,900
It's not a real fast car, it's not real slow.

73
00:05:06,901 --> 00:05:10,200
It's kind of in the middle.

74
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:13,740
Electric traction elevators are the most prevalent.

75
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:17,680
They're called traction elevators because it's traction between the motor shiv and the

76
00:05:17,681 --> 00:05:22,660
cables that lifts and lowers the car.

77
00:05:22,661 --> 00:05:29,980
Okay, we are in elevator 17 machine room.

78
00:05:29,981 --> 00:05:32,320
This is on top of the hoistway.

79
00:05:32,321 --> 00:05:36,860
This is our hoist motor, which we have the driver under here with the hoist ropes on

80
00:05:36,861 --> 00:05:37,860
it.

81
00:05:37,861 --> 00:05:42,940
As it spins, it picks the car up and down, counterweight up and down.

82
00:05:42,941 --> 00:05:47,300
The counterweight on this elevator weighs about 12,000 pounds.

83
00:05:47,301 --> 00:05:52,260
That's the weight of the car, plus 50% of the car filled to capacity.

84
00:05:52,261 --> 00:05:55,600
This helps ease the amount of work required by the hoist motor.

85
00:05:55,601 --> 00:06:02,920
Okay, right now we're roping up our cars using this elevator steel cable.

86
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:09,160
We're starting now with the spools up at the very top, letting in one dead end all the way down.

87
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:14,860
Feeding it through our counterweight shiv.

88
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,780
Then we're attaching it with a rope.

89
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:24,460
And then they'll pull it all the way back up to the very top where they put a shackle on it and hitch it up to the hitch plate.

90
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:28,940
The Caltrans job is going pretty smoothly.

91
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,440
Then, one of the elevators hits a bump.

92
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:42,760
After successfully roping eight elevators,

93
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:44,660
the ropes from one have unexpectedly

94
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:45,760
jumped off their shiv.

95
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,700
Dangling over an open shaft, the engineers

96
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,000
try to sort out the tangled ropes

97
00:06:55,001 --> 00:06:56,700
and get them back on the ship.

98
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,560
Okay, this is one.

99
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:01,460
Yes.

100
00:07:01,461 --> 00:07:02,161
This is two.

101
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:03,720
Okay.

102
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:07,140
Okay, establish the next one.

103
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:07,860
Wait, wait, wait.

104
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:09,760
This one I'm shaking.

105
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:10,560
Look where I'm shaking.

106
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:11,440
This one is one.

107
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:13,080
This one is one.

108
00:07:13,081 --> 00:07:14,920
Okay, let me spell it out to you.

109
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:17,200
Grab the number one right here.

110
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,680
Put number two right here

111
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:22,820
so I can get one and two in order here.

112
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:24,500
Because I can't see it.

113
00:07:24,501 --> 00:07:26,960
The engineers worked two days to sort out the problem.

114
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,100
It was quite a project to fix it.

115
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:31,680
We did have it all back up and running again.

116
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:33,560
We noticed that the ropes were damaged.

117
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,320
So actually, we have started replacing them.

118
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:38,840
We have to put all new ropes in the car.

119
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:44,740
Besides the hoist motor, the elevator computer, or controller,

120
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:48,080
is also located in the machine room, above the shaft.

121
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:50,740
This here is our controller.

122
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,340
This is the brains of the elevator.

123
00:07:53,341 --> 00:07:54,480
It tells it what to do.

124
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,080
It monitors what it is doing.

125
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:00,500
The computer tells the car what floor to go to,

126
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:02,360
when to open the door,

127
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:06,520
and on some elevators, how much weight just stepped onto the elevator.

128
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:09,980
And believe it or not,

129
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,920
the door close button is actually connected to the computer.

130
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:18,860
The controller also regulates the speed of the car.

131
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:22,940
One of the biggest fears people have about riding in an elevator

132
00:08:22,941 --> 00:08:25,740
is a sudden uncontrolled freefall.

133
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:32,560
It's simply the misconception or misperception we have of elevators

134
00:08:32,561 --> 00:08:37,800
from television programs and movies where the cables are constantly breaking

135
00:08:37,801 --> 00:08:41,520
and the car plummets sort of helplessly down to the shaft.

136
00:08:41,521 --> 00:08:45,900
That is almost impossible given modern safety devices.

137
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:50,900
One of those devices is the governor, which monitors the speed of the car.

138
00:08:50,901 --> 00:08:57,500
If the car were to fall and go faster than it's supposed to, it will recognize that and

139
00:08:57,501 --> 00:09:02,040
stop the shift from spinning, and it will stop this cable from spinning.

140
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:09,400
There are also safety brakes under the elevator car, which clamp down on the rails to stop

141
00:09:09,401 --> 00:09:10,720
the car in the event of a fall.

142
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:16,480
If all else fails, there's even a cushion in the bottom of the pit.

143
00:09:16,481 --> 00:09:21,480
So we have a hydraulic buffer here.

144
00:09:21,481 --> 00:09:29,360
If the elevator were to fall down the hoistway for a short distance, this hydraulic oil buffer

145
00:09:29,361 --> 00:09:30,360
will catch the car.

146
00:09:30,361 --> 00:09:31,360
This thing compresses.

147
00:09:31,361 --> 00:09:37,160
The faster the car comes, the faster it will stop the car.

148
00:09:37,161 --> 00:09:41,460
With all the safety mechanisms, there's really no need to worry about an elevator plummeting

149
00:09:41,461 --> 00:09:43,740
down a shaft.

150
00:09:43,741 --> 00:09:46,700
But that hasn't always been the case.

151
00:09:46,701 --> 00:09:52,740
Elevators used to be slow and sometimes dangerous, yet luxurious.

152
00:09:52,741 --> 00:10:04,460
Still, this economically uplifting invention would change the rules of real estate.

153
00:10:04,461 --> 00:10:10,520
The observatory elevators of the Sears Tower in Chicago travel 1,600 feet per minute.

154
00:10:10,521 --> 00:10:17,520
Because of the buildings sway, they are run slower on extremely windy days.

155
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:29,520
Don't take you eight hours to do a day's work, does it?

156
00:10:29,521 --> 00:10:32,520
Some people are professional man-lip raglers.

157
00:10:32,521 --> 00:10:35,520
I mean, you have those on every job.

158
00:10:35,521 --> 00:10:39,520
But you can tell, the lazy ones can't walk down one floor.

159
00:10:39,521 --> 00:10:40,221
One floor.

160
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:40,980
Here we go.

161
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:43,600
Down one.

162
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:49,800
When I was your age, I used to climb the ladder barefooted,

163
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:52,200
and the ladder was linked to the barbed wire, so I wouldn't slip.

164
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:56,960
After 25 years of ups and downs as a man lift operator,

165
00:10:57,620 --> 00:10:59,320
Bobby's retiring in seven weeks.

166
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:01,980
I think Bobby's great for morale,

167
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:03,940
because you come in all tired and grumpy,

168
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:06,700
and he'll make sure that you smile, say hello to everyone.

169
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:08,160
Did you say good morning to everybody?

170
00:11:08,161 --> 00:11:09,380
Good morning, everybody.

171
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:16,520
Come in a bad mood, Bobby's right here, picks you up.

172
00:11:16,521 --> 00:11:18,520
Look happy now, smile.

173
00:11:18,521 --> 00:11:19,520
Let's go get him.

174
00:11:19,521 --> 00:11:23,620
Although it might seem like Bobby's been running the man lift since the elevator was

175
00:11:23,621 --> 00:11:32,700
invented, mechanized freight hoists actually date back to the late 1700s.

176
00:11:32,701 --> 00:11:39,020
English textile mills were among the first businesses to climb aboard.

177
00:11:39,021 --> 00:11:43,000
steam engines or water wheels provided the lifting power to move goods and products.

178
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:52,580
The benefits of elevators were clear, but several deaths caused by freight hoist accidents

179
00:11:52,581 --> 00:11:56,100
proved elevators weren't yet safe for transporting passengers.

180
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:01,860
Enter American inventor Elisha Graves Otis.

181
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:08,060
In 1852, Otis installed the first hoist with a safety brake in a factory.

182
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:15,120
The following year, Otis demonstrated his new invention to the public at the Crystal

183
00:12:15,121 --> 00:12:19,340
Palace Exposition in New York.

184
00:12:19,341 --> 00:12:24,700
He would have the platform lifted up and Otis would be standing on it and then someone would

185
00:12:24,701 --> 00:12:30,200
cut the hoisting cable and the car would fall and then his improved brake would catch and

186
00:12:30,201 --> 00:12:34,580
stop the elevator car and he would be safe.

187
00:12:34,581 --> 00:12:38,400
Spectators were astonished.

188
00:12:38,401 --> 00:12:44,360
The Otis safety brake consisted of two wrought iron catches on the car and cast iron teeth

189
00:12:44,361 --> 00:12:47,440
on the guide rails.

190
00:12:47,441 --> 00:12:53,240
If for some reason the hoist rope broke or slipped, a spring tripped the catches, forcing

191
00:12:53,241 --> 00:12:59,740
them into the teeth on the sides of the shaft, stopping the fall.

192
00:12:59,741 --> 00:13:06,400
This simple but ingenious device made elevators safer for moving passengers.

193
00:13:06,401 --> 00:13:09,240
Soon, the first passenger elevators started appearing

194
00:13:09,241 --> 00:13:13,280
in department stores and luxury hotels.

195
00:13:13,281 --> 00:13:16,160
The elevator's introduced as another luxury item,

196
00:13:16,161 --> 00:13:19,840
literally as a movable room with upholstered seats,

197
00:13:19,841 --> 00:13:22,840
mirrors, chandeliers, and operator.

198
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:25,300
And it was that gracious moving room

199
00:13:25,301 --> 00:13:29,640
that carried patrons of the hotel from floor to floor.

200
00:13:29,641 --> 00:13:31,300
But the elevator quickly changed

201
00:13:31,301 --> 00:13:34,800
from luxury convenience to economic necessity.

202
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:39,680
The new invention now made taller buildings feasible,

203
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:43,040
altering the economics of real estate forever.

204
00:13:44,580 --> 00:13:47,060
Developers soon realized the new elevating device

205
00:13:47,061 --> 00:13:49,080
could also elevate office rents.

206
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:51,640
Nineteenth century New York,

207
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:53,680
primary mode of transportation was horses.

208
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:56,680
Because of not only the horses, but other activities,

209
00:13:57,220 --> 00:13:59,640
there was a lot of dirt, there was a lot of noise,

210
00:13:59,641 --> 00:14:04,060
The smells and being above that had an obvious advantage.

211
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:07,880
With an efficient means of getting people to upper stories,

212
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:11,220
the top floors, which previously had been less desirable,

213
00:14:11,660 --> 00:14:13,960
were suddenly the most valuable space.

214
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:18,300
The first commercial office building to employ an elevator

215
00:14:18,301 --> 00:14:21,540
is the Equitable Building in New York City, completed in 1870.

216
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:25,880
The trip from the ground floor to the ninth floor, the top story,

217
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:28,680
took just under two minutes.

218
00:14:28,681 --> 00:14:34,920
The business community was highly skeptical because this was a slow and gracious device

219
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:38,360
placed into the fast-paced world of American business.

220
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:42,320
There was a recognition that the elevator had to change if it was going to be successful.

221
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:48,500
And it doesn't take too long before the seats begin to disappear and the elevator starts to move much faster.

222
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:54,960
Over the next 60 years, not only did elevators become faster, but safer and more reliable.

223
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,420
Electric traction motors took the place of steam engines.

224
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:04,840
The first push-button automatic elevators started appearing in the 1920s,

225
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:08,040
making elevator operators obsolete.

226
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:13,300
There was a surprising amount of skepticism about operatorless elevators.

227
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:16,280
People did not want to run them themselves.

228
00:15:16,460 --> 00:15:18,780
They viewed this as too complicated,

229
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:23,780
which sounds very strange to us because they looked the same.

230
00:15:23,781 --> 00:15:27,040
Many elevators had buttons very similar to today.

231
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:32,160
Today's elevators are a far cry from the slow, moving rooms of the past.

232
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:38,280
Elevatoring is probably the most important thing for a very tall building.

233
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:41,860
If you don't get the people up there, you don't have a building.

234
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:49,060
Builders of the new Taipei 101 in Taiwan faced greater challenges than normal

235
00:15:49,061 --> 00:15:53,760
when designing the ultra-sophisticated elevator systems for the world's tallest building.

236
00:15:53,780 --> 00:16:02,540
In a structure this large, just getting people in, out, up and down presents unique challenges.

237
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:13,020
There will be 12,000 people working in the building after it's completed.

238
00:16:13,460 --> 00:16:19,440
We know it's important to transport these people to the floor they intend to go to in a short time.

239
00:16:19,441 --> 00:16:28,480
Therefore, we divided the vertical transportation system into three zones in order to make transportation

240
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:30,680
go more smoothly.

241
00:16:30,681 --> 00:16:35,620
Also, the elevators are double-deck elevators.

242
00:16:35,621 --> 00:16:41,480
This doubled the efficiency of vertical transportation.

243
00:16:41,481 --> 00:16:47,820
The Taipei 101 utilizes 61 elevators.

244
00:16:47,821 --> 00:16:52,820
The larger double-decker elevators can carry 62 people at a time.

245
00:16:56,820 --> 00:17:00,820
We also have two of the world's fastest elevators.

246
