480
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:23,440
His device both cooled the air and controlled the humidity.

481
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:31,380
His subsequent work formed the basis for air conditioning that is still used today.

482
00:35:31,381 --> 00:35:37,380
Movie theaters were among the first large structures to adopt the new technology.

483
00:35:37,381 --> 00:35:44,380
However, the expense discouraged new office buildings from quickly following suit.

484
00:35:44,381 --> 00:35:53,380
One of the first high-rises to employ air conditioning was the 38-story Philadelphia Savings Fund Society, or PSFS, building.

485
00:35:53,381 --> 00:36:02,980
Howell and Lescaz in Philadelphia in 1932 designed the PSFS building, which is the first

486
00:36:02,981 --> 00:36:05,991
modern fully air conditioned structure.

487
00:36:05,992 --> 00:36:09,620
Air conditioning continues to develop, but it's

488
00:36:09,621 --> 00:36:19,340
that moment when the tall building is free to be completely sealed up.

489
00:36:19,341 --> 00:36:25,180
Beyond not having operable windows, the PSFS building had dropped ceilings to conceal ductwork.

490
00:36:26,600 --> 00:36:32,320
It had mechanical rooms on several floors to reduce the space required for vertical ducts.

491
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:41,180
Engineers even hid the building's unsightly cooling equipment behind the distinctive PSFS sign on the roof.

492
00:36:43,460 --> 00:36:48,460
Even the Empire State Building, constructed just a couple of years before the PSFS building,

493
00:36:48,461 --> 00:36:51,200
didn't have air conditioning until 1950.

494
00:36:53,860 --> 00:36:57,600
The pioneering techniques first employed by the PSFS building

495
00:36:57,601 --> 00:36:58,960
are still being used today.

496
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:05,700
The buildings constructed after World War II

497
00:37:05,701 --> 00:37:08,980
presented even greater challenges for climate control engineers.

498
00:37:09,900 --> 00:37:12,960
The tremendous use of glass as a popular cladding

499
00:37:12,961 --> 00:37:15,580
made keeping interiors cool more difficult.

500
00:37:15,581 --> 00:37:25,580
Fortunately, the development of more efficient HVAC systems, tinted glass, and window coatings

501
00:37:25,581 --> 00:37:30,080
ensured occupants wouldn't bake inside the glass-encapsulated buildings.

502
00:37:32,580 --> 00:37:37,980
At our building in Los Angeles, the first truckload of air conditioning equipment has arrived.

503
00:37:39,280 --> 00:37:41,920
The building's about to get a whole lot cooler.

504
00:37:41,921 --> 00:37:54,320
Designed by Frank Lloyd Wright and completed in 1906, the Larkin Building in Buffalo, New York, was one of the first office buildings to employ mechanical cooling.

505
00:37:54,321 --> 00:38:08,321
At our Caltrans building, the Scott Company is the mechanical contractor responsible for

506
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:15,280
installing the systems the mechanical engineer has designed.

507
00:38:15,281 --> 00:38:21,020
To cool the building, Scott Company will install three massive carrier tillers, the largest

508
00:38:21,021 --> 00:38:24,000
being 30,000 pounds.

509
00:38:24,001 --> 00:38:25,640
This is the mechanical room.

510
00:38:25,641 --> 00:38:29,680
This is where, in my opinion, the heart of this building lies.

511
00:38:29,681 --> 00:38:34,680
These are chillers which, if you were to think about it, are giant air conditioners.

512
00:38:34,681 --> 00:38:38,940
All of the cooling abilities of this building basically come from these three units right

513
00:38:38,941 --> 00:38:39,940
here.

514
00:38:39,941 --> 00:38:44,580
The water is pumped into these chillers, is cooled, forced out to different areas of this

515
00:38:44,581 --> 00:38:49,260
building to keep its occupants cool.

516
00:38:49,261 --> 00:38:54,340
This process chills water to 44 degrees.

517
00:38:54,341 --> 00:38:56,700
The chilled water is then pumped to air handlers.

518
00:38:56,701 --> 00:38:59,100
This is the air handling unit.

519
00:38:59,101 --> 00:39:03,260
Basically what happens is the cooled water from the chillers or the heated water from

520
00:39:03,261 --> 00:39:06,240
the boilers comes into these connections.

521
00:39:06,241 --> 00:39:10,020
And over here we have a coil.

522
00:39:10,021 --> 00:39:14,820
The water runs into this, through this coil, through these loops in this coil, whether

523
00:39:14,821 --> 00:39:16,880
it be hot water or cold water.

524
00:39:16,881 --> 00:39:23,260
And the air, which comes from this fan over here, after it is drawn from a series of filters,

525
00:39:23,261 --> 00:39:27,980
comes over this way, goes over this coil, and the air is either heated or cooled, and

526
00:39:27,981 --> 00:39:33,500
goes through the building to provide either cold air or warm air for comfort.

527
00:39:33,501 --> 00:39:39,820
The ductwork routes the air and takes it to where it needs to go.

528
00:39:39,821 --> 00:39:42,380
Steve Hinson is handling the installation of the ductwork.

529
00:39:46,880 --> 00:39:53,880
All the ducat has numbers.

530
00:39:53,881 --> 00:40:00,720
Each one of these pieces has a number on them, and that number correlates with the drawing

531
00:40:00,721 --> 00:40:02,880
exactly where that piece goes.

532
00:40:02,881 --> 00:40:09,880
Scott Company will eventually run 20 miles of ducats.

533
00:40:09,881 --> 00:40:17,720
This is actually the biggest job I've ever run.

534
00:40:17,721 --> 00:40:19,720
It's a real big job.

535
00:40:19,721 --> 00:40:24,780
And being that a lot of guys really don't think I should be running a job because of how young I am.

536
00:40:24,781 --> 00:40:25,700
I'm only 30.

537
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:27,940
All eyes are on me right now.

538
00:40:28,040 --> 00:40:30,020
This job will either make me or break me.

539
00:40:30,420 --> 00:40:31,620
And hopefully it makes me.

540
00:40:31,621 --> 00:40:41,240
The three massive chillers that will cool the structure will also generate a tremendous

541
00:40:41,241 --> 00:40:45,280
amount of heat as a byproduct.

542
00:40:45,281 --> 00:40:49,980
So Scott Company is installing four cooling towers on the roof to take heat away from

543
00:40:49,981 --> 00:40:53,020
the chillers.

544
00:40:53,021 --> 00:40:58,280
Water circulates around a chiller to draw heat out of the machine.

545
00:40:58,281 --> 00:41:03,900
This condenser water, which is now hot, is pumped via 18-inch pipes to equipment on the

546
00:41:03,901 --> 00:41:04,900
roof.

547
00:41:04,901 --> 00:41:08,900
Okay, what I'm working on now is condenser water piping.

548
00:41:08,901 --> 00:41:15,900
Well, one of these 18-inch pipes is about 90 minutes each.

549
00:41:15,901 --> 00:41:21,660
They're visually inspected by a state inspector and they can also be x-rayed if it's deemed

550
00:41:21,661 --> 00:41:22,660
necessary.

551
00:41:22,661 --> 00:41:36,661
Basically what we have here is the fan section of the cooling tower, or the top section.

552
00:41:38,220 --> 00:41:42,800
The water comes in here on this PVC and is distributed amongst these nozzles.

553
00:41:42,801 --> 00:41:46,420
The nozzles spray the water out in a light pattern.

554
00:41:46,421 --> 00:41:52,620
A fan pulls air up through the saturated baffles, cooling the water.

555
00:41:52,621 --> 00:41:59,620
The cooled water is then sent back down to the chillers to be recycled through the system.

556
00:41:59,621 --> 00:42:06,620
To provide heating in the building, the Scott Company is installing three boilers.

557
00:42:06,621 --> 00:42:13,620
The boilers heat water, which is then pumped throughout the building to variable air volume boxes.

558
00:42:13,621 --> 00:42:18,620
Each VAV box services four or five offices.

559
00:42:18,621 --> 00:42:27,160
Inside the VAV boxes, coils transfer the heat from the water to the air, which is then forced

560
00:42:27,161 --> 00:42:34,220
into the offices via the same ductwork as the air-conditioned air.

561
00:42:34,221 --> 00:42:38,300
But creating a comfortable environment is about more than heating and cooling.

562
00:42:38,301 --> 00:42:44,660
The V in HVAC stands for ventilation.

563
00:42:44,661 --> 00:42:48,580
is a very important aspect of the design of mechanical systems.

564
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:57,020
Ventilation is the process through which you intentionally bring in air from the outside

565
00:42:57,021 --> 00:43:03,580
in order to remove or displace the pollutants that exist within a building,

566
00:43:04,100 --> 00:43:09,480
pollutants like carbon dioxide and ozone and formaldehyde and other substances like that.

567
00:43:09,481 --> 00:43:15,620
The air handler units bring in fresh air and mix it with return air from the building.

568
00:43:17,100 --> 00:43:19,820
Duct work then carries the conditioned air into the offices.

569
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:27,640
So we finally outfitted this empty shell with elevators, cooling equipment.

570
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:30,820
They don't belong here. Just throw the s*** out. Look at all that stuff.

571
00:43:30,821 --> 00:43:32,040
All right. Just throw it out.

572
00:43:32,560 --> 00:43:33,640
And hot tempers.

573
00:43:33,641 --> 00:43:41,140
The complex labyrinth of shafts, pipes and ducts connecting every sector of the building

574
00:43:41,141 --> 00:43:46,020
like a giant web is representative of the diversity of experts it takes to create a

575
00:43:46,021 --> 00:43:53,980
safe, convenient and comfortable human environment.

576
00:43:53,981 --> 00:43:57,980
And one of those experts who helped get this building up is retiring today.

577
00:43:57,981 --> 00:43:58,980
Have a good day there.

578
00:43:58,981 --> 00:44:01,400
Enjoy your ups and downs with all the guys.

579
00:44:01,401 --> 00:44:02,400
Take care of them.

580
00:44:02,401 --> 00:44:03,400
Take care of the car.

581
00:44:03,401 --> 00:44:10,840
It's a fundamental belief of ours that great buildings are made out of the unselfish collaboration of all the different parts.

582
00:44:12,300 --> 00:44:17,300
Watching the building go from the ground all the way to the top and meeting a bunch of good people on it,

583
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:21,800
looking back, you know, to be able to tell my family, hey, you see that big tall building?

584
00:44:22,520 --> 00:44:24,760
Your dad worked on it. It's exciting.

585
00:44:25,900 --> 00:44:30,620
From now on, the Caltrans building will have to manage its own ups and downs.

